    "title": "Bay County Health Department confirms another case of rabies - NewsBreak",
    "text": "BAY COUNTY, Fla. ( WMBB ) – Bay County Health Department officials say they’ve confirmed another case of rabies, this time in a dog that was already dead. Someone found the dog on July 11th in the 1200 block of Martin Luther King Jr. Boulevard. Test results showed the animal was positive for rabies. Health officials also say the animal had not been vaccinated against rabies. They issued a rabies alert for the next 60 days in the area surrounded by Baldwin on the north side, Business 98 on the south side, East Avenue on the east side and Frankford Avenue to the west. People need to be aware that rabies may currently be present in the wild animal population. Here are the steps you and your family should take to avoid rabies exposure. Avoid contact with wild or stray domesticated animals. Report any animal you notice that’s acting strangely or aggressively. Make sure you keep your own pets vaccinated against rabies. If your pet gets into a fight with a wild animal immediately report the incident to your county health department. And if you or a family member are bitten by a wild animal seek medical attention immediately. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to mypanhandle.com.",
